  "body": "Found in the review of PR #2705 (issue #2561). The PR body of PR #2705 also names it.\n\nWhat happens:\n- `crates/motion/src/machining2d/contour.rs` (about line 193 on the PR branch) calls `contour_start::lead_held(p, poly[0], &lead)` for the first keep when the user picked a start point.\n- On a tabbed level, `tab_bridge::moved_start` can move the first keep past the pick, because the first tab grows over the picked point. Then `poly[0]` is not the picked point.\n- If no lead-in fits at the moved point, `lead_held` refuses the program. The message says `start point (x, y): no lead-in fits ...` with the moved point, not the point that the user picked.\n- The automatic seam law does not refuse a point that is not picked. Thus a legal program can be refused, and the message names a point that the user did not pick.\n\nThis is not a silent wrong output. The result is a refusal or a misleading message.\n\nNot proven by a test. The review read the code only.\n\nExpected:\n- Apply `lead_held` only when the first keep starts at the picked point.\n- When the start moved, use the lead law of a keep that starts after a tab, and keep the `tabs:` note that names the move.\n\nDone means: a test with a picked start that the first tab covers and a lead-in that does not fit at the moved point. The test shows that the program is not refused with the wrong point, or that the refusal names the correct point and the cause.\n",
